.TH WRITE 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
write \- send a message to another user's terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B write
.I user
.RI [ tty ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B write
reads lines from standard input and relays them, preceded by an
announcing banner, to
.IR user 's
terminal, until end of file, at which point a final
"\fBEOT\fR"
line is sent. The banner has the form:
.RS
.PP
Message from \fIuser\fR (\fItty\fR) [\fItimestamp\fR]...
.RE
.SH REAL BEHAVIOR
There being exactly one real user identity on this system,
.I user
is only ever accepted when it names that same user, as verified
against the password database; no other name is ever "logged in" here
to write to. Correspondingly, the only terminal this process can ever
honestly claim to reach is its own controlling terminal, found by
checking, in order, standard input, standard output, and standard
error, for the first descriptor that is really a terminal. So
.RB \` "write $(whoami)" \'
degenerately writes right back into the same session it is run from.
If a
.I tty
operand is given, it must name that same terminal (the short form
.BR tty (1)
itself would print); any other
.I tty
name, or any
.I user
other than the current one, is reported as not logged in, matching
.BR write (1p) 's
own specified behavior for a session that cannot be found. This is
intentionally not a permanent restriction: were a multi\-session
registry ever added to this system, only the search for a target
terminal would need to widen, not this utility's fundamental
mechanism of writing straight into the terminal it finds.
.PP
Interrupting
.B write
while it is relaying standard input does not send an early
.B EOT
trailer and exit the way
.BR write (1p)
describes; an interrupt during that read behaves the same way it would
in any other utility blocked reading from a terminal.
.PP
.BR mesg (1) 's
permission bit is deliberately not consulted here: it gates messages
arriving from other users, and in the self\-write case described
above, the only real recipient is this same session.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I user
The user to write to. Only the invoking user's own name is ever
accepted.
.TP
.I tty
The terminal to write to, disambiguating between a user's several
open terminals. On this system, this can only ever be the sender's own
terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The message was relayed to standard input's end of file.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error (wrong number of operands), the named
.I user
is not logged in (any name other than the invoking user, or no
terminal of the invoking user's own could be found at all), the given
.I tty
does not match the invoking user's own terminal, or a failure occurred
opening or writing to the terminal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mesg (1),
.BR tty (1)
